WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.000
A Kryptonian scientist, the "Days of Our&nbsp;
Lives" inspiration, and a young Barry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.760
Allen. While these DC actors are no longer&nbsp;
with us, their performances will live on.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.600
When it comes to daily newspaper editors who&nbsp;
unknowingly work alongside world-famous crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.360
fighters, J. Jonah Jameson of The Daily&nbsp;
Bugle tends to overshadow Perry White of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.160
The Daily Planet. That's got nothing to do&nbsp;
with either of their journalistic abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.640
of course. J.J.'s just a larger-than-life&nbsp;
personality, and essentially one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.840
villains in many Spider-Man stories. On the other&nbsp;
hand, Perry White's such a diehard print media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.840
professional and advocate, he's been played by&nbsp;
many more actors than J.J., who's principally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.920
associated with the version played by J.K.&nbsp;
Simmons in Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man" trilogy.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.120
"He is, in my 40 years in this business,&nbsp;
the fastest typist I've ever seen."

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.840
Although he's not the only live-action Perry&nbsp;
White, the Superman world's answer to Simmons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.000
is probably Jackie Cooper, who corrects&nbsp;
Clark Kent's spelling errors in "Superman",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.560
"Superman II", "Superman III", and&nbsp;
"Superman IV: The Quest for Peace".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.800
Despite a major role in one of the most&nbsp;
successful film franchises of the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.200
Cooper arguably never surpassed his&nbsp;
career zenith at the age of nine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.240
when he became the youngest actor ever nominated&nbsp;
for Best Actor at the 1931 Academy Awards.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.040
But you could also argue that acting steadily in&nbsp;
film and TV from the early '30s up until 1990 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.200
a bigger accomplishment than any single role or&nbsp;
nomination. Cooper died in 2011 at the age of 88.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.320
"Easy Miss, I've got you.&nbsp;
You've got me, who's got you?"

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.120
The tragically short nature of Christopher&nbsp;
Reeve's life has been widely publicized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.240
but when Margot Kidder passed away in May&nbsp;
2018 at 69, we still felt it was too soon.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.040
Arguably, no major DC character has evolved and&nbsp;
improved over the decades more than Lois Lane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.400
Once a bumbling, air-headed klutz in constant&nbsp;
need of rescue, today's Lois is a world-famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.760
journalist and media personality, generally&nbsp;
depicted as a savvier operator than her Kryptonian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.160
husband. Now and again, Lois does her own fair&nbsp;
share of meta-human adjacent crime fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.680
albeit in a far less conspicuous capacity&nbsp;
than her spouse or most of his colleagues.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.480
Kidder appears in all the "Superman" films&nbsp;
co-starring Reeve, and we need only focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:00.000
on "Superman II" for examples of Kidder helping&nbsp;
undo the perception of Lois as a mere damsel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.880
distress. Kidder's Lois is not oblivious; she's&nbsp;
occasionally reckless, but only when journalistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.240
truth is at stake. Unlike previous iterations,&nbsp;
Kidder's Lois figures out that her bumbling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.920
co-worker and the literal benevolent space god&nbsp;
that follows her around happen to be the same guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.800
setting a precedent for future Lois Lanes who&nbsp;
don't buy that silly glasses disguise either.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.560
The quartet of Batman films starting with&nbsp;
"Batman" and ending with "Batman and Robin"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.960
can feel a little disjointed. Beginning in a&nbsp;
gloomy, quirky iteration of Gotham directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.600
by Tim Burton and ending with a candy-coated&nbsp;
cartoon fever dream imagined by Joel Schumacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.920
the series foregoes a solid sense of&nbsp;
consistency ... except when it comes to Alfred.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.680
"Well, I think I've embarrassed&nbsp;
him for long enough."

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.040
Veteran British actor Michael Gough holds down&nbsp;
the fort as Batman's congenial butler Alfred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.760
Pennyworth in all four films, including "Batman&nbsp;
Returns" and "Batman Forever". As is more or less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.440
customary for Alfred, Gough's version functions as&nbsp;
a voice of reason throughout Batman's adventures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.920
While he's more than happy to help Bruce and his&nbsp;
sidekicks save the world, he'd sleep a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.000
easier if his surrogate son could just get set&nbsp;
up with a nice girl and hang up the bat costume.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.560
Gough's TV, film, and theater career started&nbsp;
in the mid-1940s, so to say there was a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.760
more to his career than his run as Alfred&nbsp;
would be a substantial understatement. Gough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.920
passed on in March 2011 at 94, roughly&nbsp;
a mere six years short of triple digits.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.480
With all due respect to Jeep Swenson, who&nbsp;
portrayed the venomed-up villain in "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.920
and Robin," it's probably fair to say audiences&nbsp;
prefer the version of Bane played by Tom Hardy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.520
in "The Dark Knight Rises." But we don't say that&nbsp;
to diminish Swenson's acting abilities. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.240
the problems with "Batman and Robin"&nbsp;
extend far beyond its presentation of Bane.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:35.400
Swenson caught more than one significant crummy&nbsp;
break during his essentially successful career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.880
in which he spent time in multiple sections&nbsp;
of the entertainment industry. His tenure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.000
with the once prominent but currently defunct&nbsp;
World Championship Wrestling might have lasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.640
longer had that organization's creative&nbsp;
department come up with a less aggressively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.880
offensive and anti-Semitic name for him&nbsp;
than "The Final Solution." Swenson died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.480
shortly after the release of "Batman&nbsp;
and Robin" in 1997, at the age of 40.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.880
You might not immediately recognize his name;&nbsp;
but we assure you, you know William Hootkins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.080
He's mentioned here specifically for his turn&nbsp;
as the corrupt Lt. Eckhardt in "Batman" and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.200
to a lesser extent, for the solemn&nbsp;
duty of performing in "Superman IV:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.640
The Quest for Peace" in the guise of&nbsp;
Harry Howler. But the character actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.440
had a few more notable credits. For instance,&nbsp;
in "Star Wars", his character, named Porkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.400
pilots a ship called an X-Wing and attacks a&nbsp;
moon-sized space station known as the Death Star.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.880
Though known for minor roles in Hollywood,&nbsp;
his reputation in London's theater scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.400
was a different story. His obituary&nbsp;
cites his ongoing starring role in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.240
play "Hitchcock Blonde" as his career's&nbsp;
greatest triumph. But for actors with as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.800
many projects under their belt as Hootkins,&nbsp;
what they're "known for" becomes a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.000
of subjectivity in some respects.&nbsp;
He died in 2005 at the age of 57.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.400
A fixture of televised comedy since her late '80s&nbsp;
stint on "Saturday Night Live," with subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.920
recurring roles on "The Simpsons" and "3rd Rock&nbsp;
From the Sun," Jan Hooks also appeared in "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:53.160
Returns". Evil businessman Max Shreck hires&nbsp;
her character to explain stuff to presumptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.880
Gotham City mayoral candidate The Penguin,&nbsp;
like why voters prefer fingers to flippers.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.480
The Tim Burton-directed sequel&nbsp;
to "Batman" is not Hooks's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.840
DC-related credit. She also plays&nbsp;
a woman who claims to be raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.880
Superman's love child on CBS-TV's&nbsp;
"Superman 50th Anniversary Special",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:13.920
a lighthearted slice of Superman ephemera.&nbsp;
Hooks passed away in 2014 at 57 years old.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:18.920
Danny DeVito's Penguin recruits a wide variety of&nbsp;
minions for his crime spree in "Batman Returns,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:22.920
although they don't all have obvious utilitarian&nbsp;
value. If you're putting together a gang to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.480
inflict the maximum amount of violence and mayhem,&nbsp;
then why bother signing up an organ grinder?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.720
Penguin's thinking may be too sophisticated for&nbsp;
us to comprehend, so we just have to take it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.120
faith that the musical accompaniment produced by&nbsp;
Vincent Schiavelli's character, plus the amusement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.160
provided by his dancing monkey, was essential&nbsp;
to Oswald Cobblepot's diabolical machinations.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.800
Beyond the confines of "Batman Returns,"&nbsp;
Schiavelli carved out a niche as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.440
the great character actors of his era with&nbsp;
turns in "One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.560
"Fast Times at Ridgemont High", and "Ghost".&nbsp;
"X-Files" fans may recognize him from the cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.240
favorite episode "Humbug," in which he guest&nbsp;
stars along with Michael J. Anderson and Jim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:02.920
Rose, founder of the circus of the same name.&nbsp;
Schiavelli passed away in 2005 at the age of 57.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:06.960
A high-ranking staff member at Arkham Asylum&nbsp;
who happens to be named "Dr. Burton" is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.080
even close to the goofiest thing in "Batman&nbsp;
Forever." But creating a toss-away character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.960
for the purposes of delivering a good-natured&nbsp;
dig at director Tim Burton, who oversaw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.240
previous two Batman films, feels like a cheeky&nbsp;
move on the part of director Joel Schumacher.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.560
We suppose it would be disappointing&nbsp;
for René Auberjonois if his only major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.840
acting work consisted of a brief scene as a gag&nbsp;
character at the end of a lesser Batman movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.080
but that's not even close to the case. Though he&nbsp;
contributed a physical or vocal performance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.040
an insane amount of TV shows, including numerous&nbsp;
DC and Marvel animated projects, Auberjonois is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:41.480
most widely remembered as Odo on "Star Trek:&nbsp;
Deep Space Nine." He died in 2019 — not long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:47.400
after the release of the critically beloved "First&nbsp;
Cow," one of his last movies — at the age of 79.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.720
Sam Lane doesn't always get to live up to his&nbsp;
full potential in live-action Superman iterations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:55.000
so in a way, it's unfortunate that we&nbsp;
must gloss over Harve Presnell's role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.520
as Lois Lane's estranged father on "Lois and&nbsp;
Clark" to discuss his more significant work.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.480
The writer-director team of Joel and Ethan Coen&nbsp;
put themselves on Hollywood's radar with "Blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.800
Simple", but they didn't demonstrate their full&nbsp;
mastery of small town murder drama until "Fargo".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.040
Used car salesman Jerry Lundegaard hires a pair&nbsp;
of hit men for an elaborate kidnapping scheme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.920
designed to scam his father-in-law. The situation&nbsp;
collapses into a tragic, bloody shambles.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:20.040
The American Film Institute once&nbsp;
ranked "Fargo" as one of the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.200
100 films of all time, and it would be&nbsp;
a completely different movie without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.400
the veteran Presnell's conjuring&nbsp;
of Wade Gustafson — Lundegaard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.560
overbearing father-in-law and would-be mark.&nbsp;
Presnell died in 2009 at the age of 75.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.760
Here's where we confess that calling&nbsp;
screen comedy legend Fred Willard a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.600
"DC actor" is insanely reductive, and&nbsp;
the same statement applies to a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.000
of entries on this list. Willard's oblivious&nbsp;
game show host-like persona has added to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.160
satirical irony of films such as "This Is&nbsp;
Spinal Tap", "Best In Show" and "Anchorman"

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.960
Willard also played the President of the&nbsp;
United States on Lois and Clark, as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.800
Deputy Mayor of Metropolis in the Superman 50th&nbsp;
Anniversary Special. Somehow, in Superman-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.680
media, Willard kept finding his way into&nbsp;
positions of political power. We'd chalk that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:07.280
up to coincidence, if we believed in coincidence.&nbsp;
Willard passed away in May 2020 at the age of 86.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.840
The single-season TV show "The Flash" from&nbsp;
the early '90s never managed to amass the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:16.200
retro-cool credibility of "Batman" from&nbsp;
1966 or "Wonder Woman" from 1975. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.640
are probably multiple reasons for that,&nbsp;
but a glance at the show's intro music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:23.400
and opening credits indicates that somebody&nbsp;
in charge wanted very much for The Flash of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.800
network television to be the Batman of Tim&nbsp;
Burton films. CBS did not have the budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.960
and Barry Allen didn't have the&nbsp;
angst to fulfill that expectation.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.400
That's kind of unfortunate for Biff&nbsp;
Manard, as he appears in 17 out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.280
the 22-episode run of "The Flash." He was&nbsp;
still able to have a more-than-respectable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.800
career and legacy outside his time as&nbsp;
Officer Michael Murphy though. Manard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:45.960
shows up in both entries of the cult sci-fi&nbsp;
series "Trancers," and acted on a score of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:51.720
television shows throughout the '80s and&nbsp;
'90s. He died in 2014 at the age of 71.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.440
"Cyborg to Aquaman, where the&nbsp;
hell are you, fish stick?"

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.720
Cyborg has enjoyed something of a live action&nbsp;
revival as of late. As played by Ray Fisher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.240
he got a heck of a lot more screen time in&nbsp;
the recently-released "Zack Snyder's Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.760
League" than he did in the original edit,&nbsp;
while an iteration played by Joivan Wade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.880
has regularly appeared on Max's "Doom&nbsp;
Patrol" series. But back in the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.360
when studio executives falsely assumed television&nbsp;
could only handle one DC-based primetime drama at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.040
a time, Lee Thompson Young was mass media's&nbsp;
one and only, flesh-and-blood Victor Stone.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.000
Though he only drops by for three hour-long&nbsp;
adventures with Clark Kent on "Smallville,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.920
Young's version of Stone gets to be&nbsp;
a co-founder of the Justice League&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.720
alongside Green Arrow. That means the&nbsp;
onetime "Friday Night Lights" and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.640
Famous Jett Jackson" star definitely set some&nbsp;
of the groundwork for Ray Fisher's tenure with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:39.600
the character. Young, unfortunately, died&nbsp;
from suicide in 2013 at the age of 29.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.200
Perhaps you already know that Rutger Hauer —&nbsp;
certainly one of the most recognized character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.200
actors in sci-fi/fantasy — died in July&nbsp;
2019. And maybe you also already know he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:53.400
played the nefarious Wayne Enterprises executive&nbsp;
credited only as "Earle" in "Batman Begins".

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.520
While we left Heath Ledger and Christopher&nbsp;
Reeve off this list because they're both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.440
highly-publicized celebrity deaths, their&nbsp;
careers are both also closely associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:05.120
with their tenures as major DC characters. Hauer,&nbsp;
on the other hand, is probably too famous for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.520
relatively minor part he plays in "Batman Begins."&nbsp;
We happen to know it's possible to watch multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.520
screenings of "Batman Begins," and still not&nbsp;
notice the classic "Blade Runner" antagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.240
Roy Batty reassuring Bruce Wayne that he's got&nbsp;
the best interests of the Wayne legacy at heart.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.480
Though Earle isn't a major part of "Batman&nbsp;
Begins," "The Dark Knight" wouldn't be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.000
same movie if Bruce Wayne had not overcome&nbsp;
a sleazy corporate ladder climber in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.240
first part of the trilogy. Ergo, we&nbsp;
can say there are two indispensable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.480
sci-fi/fantasy/action franchises that&nbsp;
would not be the same today without Hauer.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.240
"Bruce, you're supposed to be dead.”

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:37.080
“Sorry to disappoint."

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.480
Heath Ledger's once-in-a-generation performance&nbsp;
as the Joker tends to gobble up a huge chunk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:44.840
the discourse when it comes to "The&nbsp;
Dark Knight," and rightfully so. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.560
it's unfortunate how matters related to the&nbsp;
Joker overshadow so many other components of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.960
Christopher Nolan's tour de force. Tom "Tiny"&nbsp;
Lister Jr. only spends a few moments onscreen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:57.520
but as the "tattooed inmate" who protects a&nbsp;
ship full of civilians by tossing the switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.960
to one of the Joker's bombs into the ocean,&nbsp;
he certainly makes those few moments count.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:05.120
Lister's poignant appearance in "The&nbsp;
Dark Knight" isn't necessarily his most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.920
impressive career accomplishment. Back&nbsp;
in the '80s, Lister went by the alias of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:13.480
"Zeus" and main-evented the 1989 "SummerSlam"&nbsp;
pay-per-view card, teaming with "Macho Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:17.480
Randy Savage against the tandem of Hulk&nbsp;
Hogan and Brutus "The Barber" Beefcake.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:20.240
The rest of Lister's resume&nbsp;
includes featured roles in "Friday",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:25.920
"The Fifth Element" and "Jackie Brown." Lister&nbsp;
passed away in December 2020 at the age of 62.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.080
Logan Williams appears as young Barry Allen in&nbsp;
flashback and time travel sequences during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:34.200
first two seasons of CW's "The Flash." He&nbsp;
also had a recurring role on the Hallmark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.400
Channel period drama "When Calls The&nbsp;
Heart," and landed his first role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:42.360
the Hallmark movie "The Color of Rain" at the&nbsp;
age of 10. He died in 2020 at 16 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.760
after a three-year battle with opioid&nbsp;
addiction. His mother Marlyse said at the time,

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:50.600
"His death is not going to be in vain. He's&nbsp;
going to help a lot of people down the road."

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:54.920
According to the Centers for Disease Control&nbsp;
and Prevention, nearly 69,000 people in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.920 --> 00:12:00.480
U.S. died from overdosing on synthetic opioids&nbsp;
in 2020. Sadly, Logan Williams was one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:05.360
statistics far too early into his life and what&nbsp;
should have been a long and promising career.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.120
"Can't you remember anything?”

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:10.664
“I remember the Alamo."

00:12:10.664 --> 00:12:13.000
[Laughter]

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.920
Though Paul Reubens' place in pop&nbsp;
culture history was assured by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.440
most beloved creation, Pee-wee Herman,&nbsp;
the actor-comedian also played a variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:23.720
of other roles, some of which trucked in&nbsp;
not-so-funny territory. Case in point:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:28.000
"Batman Returns," which reunited Reubens with&nbsp;
"Pee-wee's Big Adventure" director Tim Burton.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.360
He played Tucker Cobblepot, a Gotham City district&nbsp;
attorney who is horrified to discover that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:37.240
infant son, Oswald, is physically disfigured.&nbsp;
Cobblepot abandons his child, who is subsequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:41.920
raised by emperor penguins at the Old Gotham&nbsp;
City Zoo and later becomes the monstrous Penguin.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:45.680
Reubens played a drug dealer in "Blow," the&nbsp;
restless ghost of a suicide victim in "Life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:50.640
During Wartime," and the strange Mr. Vargas in&nbsp;
five episodes of "The Blacklist." He also played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.360
Elijah Van Dahl, a more sympathetic version&nbsp;
of the Penguin's father, on three episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:59.000
of "Gotham". He voiced several DC characters,&nbsp;
including Bat-Mite and the Riddler, on "Batman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:03.720
The Brave and the Bold" and the "Robot Chicken&nbsp;
DC Comics Specials", respectively. Reubens' death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:10.240
from cancer at the age of 70 on July 30, 2023,&nbsp;
was mourned by fans and collaborators alike.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.720
Though numerous actors have attempted to put&nbsp;
their stamp on the physicality of Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:18.200
one performer largely defined the Dark Knight's&nbsp;
voice for more than three decades. Voice actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:22.600
Kevin Conroy debuted as Batman in Bruce Timm&nbsp;
and Eric Radomski's Emmy-winning "Batman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:26.880
The Animated Series" in 1992 and continued&nbsp;
to voice the character in dozens and dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.720
of projects, including other&nbsp;
DC Animated Universe series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:33.600
feature films and video games. Conroy&nbsp;
also played the Earth-99 variant of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.520
Bruce Wayne on the CW's live-action Arrowverse&nbsp;
crossover event "Crisis on Infinite Earths."

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.400
Conroy, who studied acting at Juilliard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:44.360
began his career in the theater and amassed a&nbsp;
sizable number of live-action television credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:49.080
including recurring roles on "Dynasty" and "Tour&nbsp;
of Duty." In addition to his many turns as Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:53.720
Conroy also lent his distinctive voice to such&nbsp;
animated series as "The Venture Bros.," "Ben 10:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:56.920
Alien Force," and "He-Man and&nbsp;
the Masters of the Universe."

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:01.600
In 2022, Conroy penned "Finding Batman," which&nbsp;
detailed how his own troubled childhood and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:07.560
experiences as a gay man informed his portrayal&nbsp;
of Batman, for "DC Pride 2022." Conroy died of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:13.320
intestinal cancer at the age of 66 on November&nbsp;
10, 2022. His final performance as Batman will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:18.520
be featured in Rocksteady Games' "Suicide&nbsp;
Squad: Kill the Justice League" in 2024.

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:23.480
Though created by Paul Dini and Bruce Timm, the DC&nbsp;
antihero Harley Quinn also owes a debt to actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:28.280
Arleen Sorkin for her iconic look, voice, and&nbsp;
attitude. Dini, who knew Sorkin from college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:32.520
saw a compilation reel of her appearances as&nbsp;
the eccentric Calliope Jones on "Days of Our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:36.320
Lives," which included a scene in which she&nbsp;
was dressed as a jester. The scene inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.040
Dini and Timm to create Harley Quinn, a&nbsp;
wise-cracking henchwoman and foil for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.080
Joker on "Batman: The Animated Series," and&nbsp;
they tapped Sorkin to voice the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:48.040
She went on to play Quinn in five other&nbsp;
animated series and numerous video games.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:52.200
"Fine! I'll show you! You'll be&nbsp;
sorry! I'll pull a big heist and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:56.240
I'll be laughing at ya. Haha! You hear? Laughin'."

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:59.560
She logged more than 400 appearances&nbsp;
on the "Days of Our Lives", as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:03.120
as recurring and guest shots on series&nbsp;
like "Duet" and "Frasier." Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:07.440
Sorkin wrote episodes of "Tiny Toon Adventures,"&nbsp;
created the short-lived sitcom "Fired Up," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:12.560
co-produced a 2010 documentary on Pakistani&nbsp;
Prime Minister Benazir Bhutto. Sorkin died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:18.120
of complications from pneumonia and&nbsp;
multiple sclerosis on August 24, 2023.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:23.320
From the mid-1960s until Tim Burton's 1989&nbsp;
film "Batman," Adam West was the on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:27.880
face and voice of Batman for a generation of&nbsp;
audiences. West played both the Caped Crusader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:33.760
and his dashing alter ego, Bruce Wayne, on&nbsp;
ABC's "Batman" series from 1966 to 1968,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:37.040
but was unable to parlay that show's&nbsp;
brief but meteoric fame into a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:41.520
substantial acting career. Like George Reeves&nbsp;
and many other actors who played superheroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.840
West found Batman to be a blessing and a&nbsp;
curse: it elevated him to stardom but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:49.160
became a glass ceiling through which&nbsp;
he was unable to break for decades.

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:53.600
Perseverance and a willingness to spoof his screen&nbsp;
persona led to West's revival as a comic actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:58.880
and voice performer in the 1990s and 2000s. He&nbsp;
appeared, often as a smug version of himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:02.840
in series like "King of Queens,” as well&nbsp;
as films like "Drop Dead Gorgeous," and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:07.134
voiced numerous characters in animated series,&nbsp;
including the deranged mayor on "Family Guy."

00:16:07.134 --> 00:16:10.040
"Ugh, what don't you have in that belt?”

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:13.560
“Patience, for harlequin hoodlums like yourself."

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:17.680
He also reteamed with "Batman" co-star Burt&nbsp;
Ward to reprise their roles in animated form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:22.800
for two features, including "Batman: Return of&nbsp;
the Caped Crusaders" in 2016. West's death from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:28.360
leukemia at the age of 88 on June 9, 2017,&nbsp;
was commemorated by the city of Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:32.440
which shone the Bat-Signal on&nbsp;
City Hall on June 15 of that year.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:37.640
Season 1, Episode 6 of "Lucifer," titled "Favorite&nbsp;
Son," introduced viewers to Hank Cutter, president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:42.360
of the Los Diablos motorcycle club, whom Lucifer&nbsp;
and Chloe suspect as the chief culprit behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:46.000
the theft of a shipping container belonging&nbsp;
to the Prince of Darkness from a warehouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.520
Hank proves to be anything but the typical biker&nbsp;
captain. Though he looks tough as nails, what Hank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:54.720
really wants to do is start his own clothing&nbsp;
line. He doesn't even like riding his bike.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:58.840
Tom Sizemore was in the midst of a comeback&nbsp;
when he appeared as Hank Cutter on "Lucifer."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:02.600
A substance abuse problem and other personal&nbsp;
issues had caused the Golden Globe and Screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:06.160
Actors Guild nominee to fall far from his&nbsp;
status as a popular character actor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:10.680
films like "Saving Private Ryan," "Heat,"&nbsp;
and "Natural Born Killers." After stints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:14.960
in rehab and jail in the 2010s, Sizemore began&nbsp;
to rebuild his screen career, largely through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:20.080
recurring roles on "Hawaii Five-0," "The Red&nbsp;
Road," and the 2017 revival of "Twin Peaks."

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:23.400
However, Sizemore was hospitalized&nbsp;
after experiencing a brain aneurysm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:26.320
and stroke at his home in&nbsp;
Los Angeles on February 18,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:31.520
2023. Doctors informed his family on February&nbsp;
27 that there was no chance of recovery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:38.160
and his family made an end-of-life decision for&nbsp;
the 61-year-old actor. He died on March 3, 2023.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:43.480
Warner Bros.' 2010 feature version of DC's weird&nbsp;
Western title "Jonah Hex" was an unmitigated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:48.280
disaster upon release. You can't place the blame&nbsp;
on its cast, however, including Lance Reddick. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:52.760
"John Wick" star plays a general store owner who&nbsp;
also supplies Hex with his heavy-duty weaponry.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:57.240
A one-time music student who released an&nbsp;
album of contemporary jazz in 2010, Reddick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.240 --> 00:18:01.840
began acting in the late 1990s and earned his&nbsp;
first big break as Baltimore police lieutenant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:06.560
and later commissioner, Cedric Daniels on "The&nbsp;
Wire." The critical success of that series led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:10.880
to steady work for Reddick in character roles,&nbsp;
often as authority figures, on series like "Lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:14.960
"Bosch," and "Resident Evil," and in films like&nbsp;
the "John Wick" tetralogy — which cast him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:20.200
the Continental's concierge, as well as "White&nbsp;
House Down" and "Godzilla vs. Kong." Reddick died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:26.720
of heart disease at the age of 60 at his home&nbsp;
in Los Angeles, California on March 17, 2023.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:28.520
"I love you, son.”

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:29.760
“Stop!"

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:34.280
British actor Julian Sands played a crucial&nbsp;
figure in Superman's mythos in Seasons 9 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:39.360
10 of "Smallville." Sands played Jor-El, the&nbsp;
Kryptonian scientist who sends his son, Kal-El,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:42.640
to Earth to avoid their planet's&nbsp;
destruction. He also transfers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:46.280
his brainwaves into his son's spacecraft,&nbsp;
providing him with the superior intellect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:50.400
to become Superman. "Smallville" was&nbsp;
not Sands' only foray into DC projects:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:54.080
he also played Dr. Gerald Crane,&nbsp;
father of the Scarecrow, on "Gotham."

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:58.560
Sands rose to fame in the 1980s playing&nbsp;
diametrically opposite romantic characters:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:02.280
Helena Bonham Carter's free-thinking love&nbsp;
interest in "A Room with a View" and a haunted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:06.520
Percy Bysshe Shelley in "Gothic." He soon&nbsp;
proved adept at a wide array of characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:10.840
from a malevolent spellcaster in "Warlock"&nbsp;
and a dashing entomologist in "Arachnophobia"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:16.000
to a vicious pimp in "Leaving Las Vegas." No&nbsp;
part proved too offbeat for Sands: he was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.360
centipede in human form in David Cronenberg's&nbsp;
"Naked Lunch," the title character in Dario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:24.960
Argento's "The Phantom of the Opera," and even&nbsp;
the voice of a cat in "Bobbleheads: The Movie."

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:27.760
Between these assignments were&nbsp;
numerous television roles, stage work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:31.920
and voiceovers for video games. An avid&nbsp;
hiker, Sands disappeared in California's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:38.280
San Gabriel Mountains in January 2023. Human&nbsp;
remains found in the area on June 24, 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:43.603
were positively identified as the 65-year-old&nbsp;
actor, whose cause of death remains undetermined.

